<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-15" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Partial Derivatives</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      For multi-variable functions, we need to take a derivative for each variable. These derivatives are called partial derivatives. Fortunately we already know all
      of the derivative rules from Math 151, so we move quickly through this section to applications in the next section.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
    <title>Partial Derivatives</title>
     <introduction>

    <p>
    In this section we will look at:
    </p>

  </introduction>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Notation for partial derivatives.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Higher-order and mixed partial derivatives.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          The chain rule
        </p>
      </li>
          </ul>
  </objectives>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-pd-notation">
    <title>Notation</title>
    <p>
      Partial derivatives are denoted with "curly" d's instead of straight d's like ordinary derivatives. So the partial derivatives with respect to <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are
      denoted as 
      <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}
      </md>
      This is can challenging to write for those with less than neat handwriting and is also difficult to typeset. An alternative, easier notation is to use the a subscript 
      for the derivatives.
      <md>
        <mrow> \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}=\,\amp f_x(x,y)</mrow>
        <mrow> \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}=\,\amp f_y(x,y)</mrow>
      </md>
      This subscript notation is also much easier for second or other higher-order derivatives.
    </p>
    <p>
      When we take a partial derivative, we simply take the derivative for the given variable while treating the other variable as a constant.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^4 y + xy^{-2}</m>. The partial derivatives are
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x = \,\amp 4x^3 y + y^{-2} </mrow>
          <mrow>f_y = \,\amp x^4 y -2xy^{-3} </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \ln(x^2+y^2)</m>. The partial derivatives are
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x = \,\amp \frac{2x}{x^2+y^2} </mrow>
          <mrow>f_y = \,\amp \frac{2y}{x^2+y^2} </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = y^2/(1+x^2)^3</m>. The partial derivatives are
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x = \,\amp \frac{-6xy^2}{(1+x^2)^4}</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y = \,\amp \frac{2y}{(1+x^2)^3} </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = y^x</m>. The partial derivatives are
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x = \,\amp \ln(x)y^x \quad\text{exponential rule since x is the variable and y is a constant}</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y = \,\amp xy^{x-1} \quad\text{power rule since y is the variable and x is a constant}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-higher-order-partials">
    <title>Mixed Partial Derivatives</title>
    <p>
      Since we have two partial derivatives for a function with two input variables we can then calculate two derivatives from each of these derivatives. Using the 
      subscript notation, we would write <m>f_{xx}</m> for the second partial derivative with respect to <m>x</m> and <m>f_{xy}</m> for the partial derivative first with
      respect to <m>x</m> and second with respect to <m>y</m>. Since we are mixing variables for this second derivative, this is called a mixed partial derivative.
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = x^3+y^2 e^x</m> Then the first and second order partial derivatives are:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x =\,\amp 3x^2+y^2 e^x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_y =\,\amp 2ye^x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xx} =\,\amp 6x+y^2 e^x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xy} =\,\amp 2ye^x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{yy} =\,\amp 2e^x</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{yx} =\,\amp 2ye^x</mrow>
        </md>
        Notice that the mixed partial derivatives are equal.
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Mixed partial derivatives are always equal. This is known as Clairaut's Theorem. Not only does <m>f_{xy}=f_{yx}</m> but other higher-order mixed partials are also 
      equal, so <m>f_{xxxy} = f_{xxyx} = f_{xyxx} = f_{yxxx}</m> (all of the mixed partials with one derivative with respect to <m>y</m> and three derivatives with respect
      to <m>y</m>).
    </p>
    <example>
      <p>
        Calculate <m>f_{yxx}</m> for the function <m>f(x,y) = \cos(x+y^2)</m>
      </p>
      <p>
        By Clairaut's theorem, we can calculate two derivatives with respect to <m>x</m> and one derivative with respect to <m>y</m> in order. For this function, it is easier
        to calculate derivatives with respect to <m>x</m>, we will start there:
        <md>
          <mrow>f_x= \amp -\sin(x+y^2)</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xx}= \amp -\cos(x+y^2)</mrow>
          <mrow>f_{xxy}= \amp -2y\cos(x+y^2) = f_{yxx}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </example>
    <p>
      Here is a video with more examples.
    </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Partial Derivative Examples (18 minutes)</caption>
      <video youtube="RVwcBGzQcT8"/>
    </figure>
