    </p>
  </paragraphs>
  </subsection>
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-pd-chain-rule">
    <title>The Chain Rule</title>
    <p>
      For the chain rule it is more convenient to use Leibniz (curly d) notation. For function <m>f(x(t),y(t))</m> where <m>t</m> is the "inside" variable. By the chain rule
      <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
      </md>
      </p>
      <example>
        <p>
          Calculate <m>\frac{\partial f}{\partial t}  </m> for the function <m>f(x,y) = 4x^2 + 3y^2</m> where <m>x(t) = \sin(t)</m> and <m>y(t) = \cos(t)</m>.
          Start be calculating the derivatives with respect to <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{\partial f}{\partial x} =\,\amp f_x = 8x </mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{\partial f}{\partial y} =\,\amp f_y = 6y </mrow>
          </md>
          Now calculate the derivatives with respect to <m>t</m>.
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{\partial x}{\partial t} =\,\amp\, \cos(t) </mrow>
            <mrow> \frac{\partial y}{\partial t} =\,\amp -\sin(t) </mrow>
          </md>
          Plugging it all into the chain rule formula gives:
          <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial t} + \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial t}
      </md>
      <md>
        = (8x)(\cos(t))+ (6y)(-\sin(t)) = 8(\sin(t))(\cos(t))-6(\cos(t))(\sin(t)) = 2\sin(t)\cos(t)
      </md>
        </p>
      </example>
      <p>
        For inside functions of two-variables the chain rule for <m>z=f(x(u,v),y(u,v))</m> is 
        <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial u} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial u} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial u}
      </md>
      and
      <md>
        \frac{\partial f}{\partial v} = \frac{\partial z}{\partial x}\frac{\partial x}{\partial v} + \frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\frac{\partial y}{\partial v}
      </md>
      </p>
       <p>
      Here is a video with more examples.
    </p>
